1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:26,000
SPEAKERB: It is. But walking only shows that the conclusion is false. It doesn't show which step of the argument fails.

2
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:56,000
SPEAKERA: Aristotle noted that shorter distances take shorter times. But then the time, too, is made of infinitely many pieces.

3
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:26,000
SPEAKERB: So his deeper answer splits infinity in two.

4
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:56,000
SPEAKERA: An actual infinity would be a completed collection of infinitely many things, all there at once.

5
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:26,000
SPEAKERB: And a potential infinity is a process that can always go on, but is never completed.

6
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:56,000
SPEAKERA: A path, he said, holds infinitely many halves only potentially. They'd be actual only if the runner stopped at each one.

7
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:26,000
SPEAKERB: So a smooth run never has to tick the halves off one by one.

8
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:56,000
SPEAKERA: In his words, if the units are actual, it is not possible; if they are potential, it is possible.

9
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:26,000
SPEAKERB: And that answer satisfied nearly all scholars for about 1,500 years.

10
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:56,000
SPEAKERA: Meanwhile, mathematicians were learning to tame infinite sums.

11
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:26,000
SPEAKERB: In the third century BC, Archimedes found the area of a slice cut from a parabola.

12
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:56,000
SPEAKERA: He filled it with triangles, and each new row of triangles has a quarter of the area of the row before.

13
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:26,000
SPEAKERB: So the slice is the big triangle, times one, plus a quarter, plus a sixteenth, and so on.

14
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:56,000
SPEAKERA: And Archimedes showed that this comes to exactly four thirds of the big triangle.

15
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:26,000
SPEAKERB: An infinite sum with a tidy answer, long before calculus.

16
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:56,000
SPEAKERA: But infinite sums can be slippery. Take one, minus one, plus one, minus one, and so on.

17
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:26,000
SPEAKERB: Pair the terms one way and you get zero. Pair them another way and you get one.

18
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:56,000
SPEAKERA: Zero and one, from the same sum?

19
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:26,000
SPEAKERB: That's the trouble. In 1821, the French mathematician Augustin Louis Koh-shee insisted on strict tests of when a sum makes sense.

20
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:56,000
SPEAKERA: Its value is the number its running totals settle down to, if they settle at all.
